{"name":"Peace of Mind","hex":"#c1875f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peace of mind","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The absence of mental turmoil or anxiety."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peace_of_mind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Inner peace","blocks":[{"text":"Inner peace refers to a deliberate state of psychological or spiritual calm maintained despite the presence of stressors. It is associated with a state of psychological \"homeostasis\" and the opposite of being stressed or anxious, and is considered to be a state where one's mind performs at an optimal level, regardless of outcomes. Peace of mind is thus generally associated with a state of contentment and emotional well-being including bliss, happiness and contentment."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inner_peace","wikidata":"Q2615915"},{"title":"Peace of Mind (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Peace of Mind was a rap rock band composed of rapper KJ-52, rock artist Pete Stewart, keyboardist/producer Todd Collins, and drummer Rick May. Songs were written and performed in hip hop form by KJ-52 before being edited by Stewart. Although the band's one self-titled album was not largely popular, it was well received among KJ-52's following. Another Peace of Mind album has been rumored, though KJ announced at his podcast that he will only consider writing another if opportunity comes, noting that his solo work is better received. The band was signed to BEC Recordings."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_of_Mind_(band)","wikidata":"Q7157534"},{"title":"Peace of Mind (Boston song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Peace of Mind\" is a song by American rock band Boston, written by Tom Scholz. It was on their 1976 debut album Boston, and was released the next year as the third and final single from the album. It peaked at number 38 on the U.S. Billboard Hot 100 in 1977, as well as number 33 on the Cash Box Top 100. It received substantial radio airplay, both upon the initial release of the Boston album and subsequently, and has been described as a \"rock-radio staple\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a2/Peace_of_Mind_%28Boston_song%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_of_Mind_(Boston_song)","wikidata":"Q3512064"},{"title":"Peace","blocks":[{"text":"Peace is a state of harmony in the absence of hostility and violence. In a societal sense, peace is commonly used to mean a lack of conflict and freedom from fear of violence between individuals or groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Peace_dove_%283329620077%29.jpg/330px-Peace_dove_%283329620077%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace","wikidata":"Q454"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG/330px-Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG/330px-Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Eirene, goddess of peace in ancient Greek religion, with the infant Plutus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg/250px-Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg/250px-Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c